                    <h2>Make glass work harder without closing the room.</h2>
                    <p>Privacy and decorative films can shape sightlines, divide interior areas, and add a deliberate
                        finish. The right direction depends on viewing angles, lighting, glass location, and the level
                        of privacy the space needs.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="commercial-section commercial-section--paper" id="process">
            <div class="commercial-shell">
                <div class="commercial-heading">
                    <p class="commercial-overline">Project process</p>
                    <h2>A site-specific path from problem to scope.</h2>
                </div>
                <ol class="commercial-process">
                    <li><span>01</span><div><h3>Share the property</h3><p>Send the city, property type, photos, rough measurements, and the primary goal.</p></div></li>
                    <li><span>02</span><div><h3>Review the glass + access</h3><p>We discuss the glazing, project areas, access, use of the space, and any details still needed.</p></div></li>
                    <li><span>03</span><div><h3>Compare an approach</h3><p>Film direction and scope are based on the actual site rather than a generic one-size-fits-all package.</p></div></li>
                    <li><span>04</span><div><h3>Confirm the next step</h3><p>After the review, we can identify what is ready for an estimate and what requires further site information.</p></div></li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </section>

